    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §396-kkk, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10092\n\nSPONSOR: BROUK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to enacting the\n\"no gambling ads for kids act\"\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Short title. This act shall be known and may be cited as the\n\"no gambling ads for kids act\"\n\n§ 2. The general business law is amended by adding a new section 396-KKK\nto read as follows:\n\n1. Defines numerous terms including add-on transaction, loot box, online\ngaming-related gambling, pay-to-win microtransaction, and predictive\nmarket wagering\n\n2. Makes it unlawful for a social media platform to promote advertise-\nments for online gaming-related gambling, online sweepstakes gaming,\nsports-related gambling, or traditional online gambling unless an opera-\ntor has reasonably determined that the user is not a minor\n\n3. Protects the data used to verify a user's age and deleted the data\nafter the age is verified\n\n4. Established that violates will be enforced by the Attorney General\n\n5. Directs the Attorney General to maintain a website to receive\ncomplaints, violations, and referrals from the public.\n\n6. Directs the Attorney General to promulgate regulations as necessary\nto effectuate the provision in this section\n\n7. Directs the gaming commission to maintain guidance for the gaming and\ngambling industry on restrictions for online advertisements to anyone\nunder 18\n\n§ 3. Creates protections if any section of the law is struck down in\ncourt\n\n§ 4. This act shall take effect on the 180th day after it shall have\nbecome a law. Effective immediately, the addition, amendment and/or\nrepeal of any rule or regulation necessary for the implementation of\nthis act on its effective date are authorized to be made and completed\non or before such effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAccording to a 2026 report issued by Common Sense Media, 61% of boys\nbetween 11 and 17 have seen gambling ads on YouTube. That same group\nreported seeing 60% on social media and 57% notice them during live or\nstreaming broadcast streams. These ads have prompted 7% of boys to\ngambling after seeing these ads.\n\nThe adolescents who are exposed to the sports betting ads on social\nmedia do not seek it out, it is arriving in their feeds through algo-\nrithmic promotion that shows up unprompted. The exposure to these ads is\nextremely dangerous because someone who is a minor does not grasp the\nrisks of gambling and is only focused on the thought of winning. Because\nof this, they are more likely to download the gambling application and\nuse a family member's ID to create an account so they can win prizes.\n\nThis legislation simply prohibits social media platforms from displaying\nany advertisement related to gambling to a minor on their platform. The\nbill will also allow the Attorney General to hold platforms accountable\nfor violating this legislation and allowing minors to be exposed to\nthese advertisements.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Legislation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the 180th day after it shall have become a\nlaw. Effective immediately, the addition, amendment and/or repeal of any\nrule or regulation necessary for the implementation of this act on its\neffective date are authorized to be made and completed on or before such\neffective date\n",
